well if we have so many examples of  unregistered sales of securities  and we have so many examples of  situations where there appears to be  significant fraud associated even with  the largest of these initial coin  offerings a reasonable question to ask  is where's the sec what are they doing  about this  it's a darn good question i wish i had a  good answer  by my count the securities and exchange  commission has brought only six  enforcement actions as of july of 2018  in a situation where there are thousands  of examples of securities fraud  violations involving billions of dollars  i've been publicly quoted as saying this  is the largest violation of the federal  securities laws since the code of  hammurabi interesting question  why is the sec doing nothing  well there's several possible  explanations and i don't know that any  of them is satisfactory  one is that the sec doesn't understand  the magnitude or the nature of this  problem  i find that hard to believe  they read the same newspapers that we do  they can read the same research reports  out of the university of pennsylvania  they can look at the same studies that  the wall street journal has done so the  possibility the sec is include in as to  the nature of these issues strikes me as  implausible  the second possibility is that  developing these cases takes time  if we look at the history of sec  enforcement actions it turns out that on  average from the time the sec begins an  investigation to the time that they  actually bring a proceeding is about 22  months  so it may be that the sec is hard at  work behind the scenes developing a  large number of these cases and that at  some point in the future we're going to  see a deluge of litigation against some  of these icos that haven't been  registered or that are fraudulent  a piece of me hopes that's the case  but i wouldn't exactly bet the farm on  it  third possibility is what i call  strategic surrender by the sec  the sec could stand back and the sec  could say wait a minute  if we brought these cases and if we  prevailed what would we get  we would typically get an injunction  that tells these people not to do it  again  and then we'd get an order that says  return the money  but if many of these people are outside  our jurisdiction  and if it's in crypto in the form of  bitcoin or ether and if we don't have  the ability to track it  then what good is the remedy  well i'm going to argue there are other  ways that we could go at these people  but i understand the sec's argument that  wait a minute  we may not have an effective remedy and  rather than pursue all of these people  in court and get hollow victories  with injunctions that we really can't  enforce we'll use our resources for  something else  another theory is that the sec says hey  wait a minute let the states deal with  it well that's interesting the states  have in fact brought approximately 70  actions  the states have been about 10 times more  active in this area than the securities  exchange commission  but i would ask wait a minute  a lot of this activity is international  a lot of it is national as a practical  matter none of it is localized to texas  or massachusetts you don't find internet  offerings limited to people in austin or  topeka  it's a national phenomenon and if it's a  national phenomenon shouldn't the sec be  on the case  other possibility resource allocation we  only have so many lawyers at the sec  there are only so many frauds that we  can chase you know at any one point in  time so we're going to follow other  things we're going to pursue other  people  that's an interesting point and the  question that i would pose to the sec is  what else are you doing that's so much  more important than pursuing these large  and obvious frauds  which are hitting many people who are  basically mom and pop on main street  don't understand what goes on in the  world in crypto and want to catch a  little piece of the magic all right you  know they want to be as rich as the  people who got in on bitcoin when it was  a nickel and it rose up to twenty  thousand dollars only now to collapse  down to six right they want a piece of  that too and many of them are losing  everything as they're chasing that dream  another possible explanation has a moral  dimension  look at what's going on here  what we have is a bunch of libertarian  crypto anarchists ripping off another  bunch of libertarian crypto anarchists  and we as the united states government  don't want to get in the middle of  one bunch of libertarian crypto  anarchists ripping off other libertarian  crypto anarchists so you guys have fun  i take a little bit of a different  approach here  from my perspective i think it's  important for the sec to assert its  jurisdiction where it legitimately  exists in this area  and if we're up to me and it's not  i'd be filing lots of actions making  relatively straightforward claims that  these are securities they should have  been registered they weren't registered  but in addition to the traditional  remedies of getting the injunction and  an order that says pay back the money  and i know the money's not coming back  i would borrow some techniques from  intellectual property lawyers that  litigate copyright cases over the  internet with great frequency  and what i would do is i'd get a  provision of the injunction  that limits the abilities  of  providers on the internet  right of hosts and of search engines to  post information relating to these  frauds  so you can get a court to issue the  equivalent of a takedown order  saying if this white paper by this  organization  violates this injunction  you then won't be able to find it if you  search for it on google  it won't be allowed to be hosted on  these different places on the internet  all right now  that doesn't stop the fraud i get that  but what it does is it makes the search  costs to find the fraud much higher  and if those search costs are increased  then the number of people who will wind  up investing in that particular fraud  will go down  i agree we're not going to be able to  stop this kind of fraud  but if we make it more difficult and  more expensive for people to perpetrate  the fraud by directing these offers into  the united states  we can take steps to protect people from  obvious scams that are out there
 

